#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158021} :activity/id #uuid "210d61c7-a60b-54cc-b31c-54be37640c94" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158585 :tutorial/content "We'll now begin discussing **relations**, one of the most important topics in elementary set theory. Most (if not all) of the mathematical concepts you've learned up to this point can be thought of in terms of relations. The goal is to give these concepts a common framework.\n\nTo start with, let's define the set $A$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{1,2,3,4\\big\\}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the following standard relationships we can define between elements of $A{:}$\n\n- Suppose we want to define the concept \"$a$ is less than $b$\" over the elements of $A$. One way to do this is to write down a set containing *all ordered* pairs $(a,b)$ such that $a < b$ and $a, b\\in A$. This gives the following set:\n$R_{<} = \\big\\{(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4), (3,4)\\big\\}$\n- Next, suppose we want to define \"$a$ divides $b$\" over the elements of $A$. This time, we write down the ordered pairs $(a,b)$ such that $a \\mid b$.\n$R_{\\,\\mid\\,} = \\big\\{(1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (1,4), (2,2), (2,4),(3,3), (4,4) \\big\\}$\nNote that $(1,1) \\in R_{\\,\\mid\\,}$ since $1 \\mid 1$. The same is true for for $(2,2),(3,3),(4,4)$.\n- Finally, let's define \"$a$ has the same parity as $b$\" over $A$. Remember that two numbers have the same parity if they're both even or both odd:\n$R_{p} = \\big\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(2,4),(3,1),(3,3), (4,2), (4,4)\\big\\}$\nNote that \"$2$ has the same parity as $4$\" and \"$4$ has the same parity as $2$\" are considered separately. Since both are true, we include $(2,4)$ *and* $(4,2)$ in our set of ordered pairs.\n\nAlthough $R_{\\,<\\,}, R_{\\,\\mid\\,}$ and $R_p$ describe very different relationships between pairs of elements of $A$, they have one thing in common: they're all *subsets of the Cartesian product* $A\\times A$. This suggests that subsets of the Cartesian product can be used to describe a wide variety of mathematical relationships that exist between pairs of set elements.\n\nThis gives us our first definition:\n\n> *A **binary relation** $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2$.*\n> $R\\subseteq A^2$\n\nWe often refer to binary relations simply as **relations**. So, the sets $R_{\\,<\\,}, R_{\\,\\mid\\,}$ and $R_p$ are all (binary) relations on $A$." :tutorial/id #uuid "04d36126-9a77-57ca-99f4-8f8b0261a263" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9025 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e7673c59-bf1b-5e34-8f2d-0ab39564370a" :step/math-academy-id 152201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157020 :question/id #uuid "40a594ed-35ce-52ed-817e-04398ad06b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-14596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "481d9abf-1edd-5049-bae7-2e727fe3954c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157665}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Standard Relation"} :step/id #uuid "0d1e6d28-aa3e-5ea5-bf06-6304ce1af188" :step/math-academy-id 152202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157021 :question/id #uuid "aea9d065-3d1c-5b65-81ed-30682fc2b528" :question/math-academy-id "e-14629"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157669}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations"} :step/id #uuid "a54bf583-fe02-5dcd-b5f6-14afd103e1ab" :step/math-academy-id 152206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158588 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a binary relation $R$ on $A$, so $R\\subseteq A^2$. Then, the following notation is often convenient:\n\n- When $(x,y) \\in R$, we write $x \\: R \\: y$. In words, we say \"$x$ is related to $y$ by $R$.\"\n- When $(x,y) \\notin R$, we write $\\require{cancel} x \\cancel{R} y$. In words, we say \"$x$ is *not* related to $y$ by $R$.\"\n\nFor example, consider the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\:: \\: x \\mid y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe following statements are equivalent:\n$(2,10) \\in R \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 2 \\: R \\: 10$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\notin R \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 3 \\: {\\cancel{\\!R\\!}} \\: 10\n$$\n\nThe relation $x \\mid y$ is a so-called **standard relation.** With standard relations, we can replace the letter $R$ with the usual symbol describing the relation to retrieve our usual notation:\n\n$$\n(2,10) \\in R \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 2 \\: {\\color{red}{R}} \\: 10 \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 2 \\:{\\color{red}{\\mid}}\\: 10\n$$\n\nFor an ordered pair that's not an element of our relation, we negate the usual symbol.\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\not\\in R \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 3 \\: {\\color{red}{\\cancel{\\!R\\!}}} \\: 10 \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 3 \\:{\\color{red}{\\not\\mid}}\\: 10\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to other standard relations. For example, consider the set $A$, given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{2,4,6,8,10\\big\\}\n$$\n\nSuppose we define the relations \"is less than\" $(\\lt)$, \"is congruent modulo $2$ to\" $(\\equiv)$, and \"is equal to\" $(=)$ over $A$. Then, we have the following equivalences.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2, 10) ∈ R_{<} & ⇔2R <10 & ⇔2<10 \\\\ (2, 4) ∈ R_{≡} & ⇔2R ≡4 & ⇔2≡4 \\\\ (6, 6) ∈ R_{=} & ⇔6R =6 & ⇔6=6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for elements not in the standard relation, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(10, 2) ∉ R_{<} & ⇔10R <2 & ⇔10≮2 \\\\ (1, 4) ∉ R_{≡} & ⇔1R ≡4 & ⇔1≢4 \\\\ (2, 4) ∉ R_{=} & ⇔2R =4 & ⇔2\\ne4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, not only can relations be thought of as a general way to describe relationships between mathematical objects, but the $a\\,R\\, b$ notation is something you've been using all along!" :tutorial/id #uuid "1841ce90-0dde-5446-89a6-c5fa3ca6ad09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9063 :tutorial/title "The x R y Notation"} :step/id #uuid "baf7b7aa-be62-53dd-808f-5f0c6537ba5f" :step/math-academy-id 152210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157022 :question/id #uuid "0b2b99c2-a0fc-5194-9139-4348ef53d50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157671}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"} :step/id #uuid "55476f9a-5abc-5a94-964e-c51e9eeb10d6" :step/math-academy-id 152211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158589 :tutorial/content "The relations we've considered so far (e.g., \"is greater than\" or \"divides\") are all standard relations that are encountered regularly when studying mathematics.\n\nHowever, defining a relation as a subset of a Cartesian product allows us to think of relations more broadly.\n\nTo explain, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{1,2,3,4\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe saw how to define \"$a$ is less than $b$,\" \"$a$ divides $b$,\" and \"$a$ has the same parity as $b$\" over $A$. But relations do not necessarily need to have any \"standard\" meaning attributed to them. For example, the following set is also a valid relation on $A{:}$\n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{(1,2), (1,4), (3,2), (4,3)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou might be hard-pressed to find an intuitive meaning behind this relation, but it's a relation on $A$ nonetheless! We'll refer to these kinds of relations as **non-standard relations.**\n\nWe can also define non-standard relations using set-builder notation. For example, consider the following relation over\n\n$$\nB = \\{2,4,5\\}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in B^2 \\:: \\: x \\mid y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in B^2$ such that $x$ divides $y^2{:}$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in B$ to see which elements $y \\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x={\\color{red}{2}}$, then the values $y={\\color{blue}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{4}}$ satisfy $x\\mid y^2$, since\n${\\color{red}{2}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{2}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{4}}^2$.\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ and $({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}})$.\n- If $x={\\color{red}{4}}$, then the values $y={\\color{blue}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{4}}$ satisfy $x\\mid y^2$, since\n${\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{4}}^2$.\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ and $({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{4}})$.\n- If $x={\\color{red}{5}}$, then the value $y={\\color{blue}{5}}$ satisfies $x\\mid y^2$, since\n${\\color{red}{5}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}^2$.\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pair $({\\color{red}{5}},{\\color{blue}{5}})$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{2}}), ({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}}), ({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}), ({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{4}}), ({\\color{red}{5}},{\\color{blue}{5}}) \\big\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying the $x\\,R\\,y$ notation to non-standard relations, care must be used when replacing $R$ with the symbol involved in the description of the relation. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\in R \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2\n$$\n\nhowever\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\in R \\quad \\not\\Leftrightarrow\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e7493ee-4971-5982-b2ab-bc30e6e1afca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9064 :tutorial/title "Non-Standard Relations"} :step/id #uuid "54b66a12-0a0e-5c07-99e2-b738a9747c50" :step/math-academy-id 152215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157238 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157019 :question/id #uuid "682caa5d-ea1c-5701-b53d-c1e7c5419006" :question/math-academy-id "e-14593"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d20eac1d-b78f-5ac7-a3b7-fc72b15307a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157782}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements of a Non-Standard Relation"} :step/id #uuid "6a99bbec-5487-5087-a404-7c7a7c1b33ad" :step/math-academy-id 152216}] :activity/title "Relations on Finite Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157475}]}
